                method: 'Rolling origin: for every target season, each model is rebuilt from the seasons before it only, by its own selection rule, then the season is scored once. Pre-game odds and the Season Simulator, 2012-13 to 2025-26: the prior constants are fitted on the seasons before, each form\'s coefficients on every game of 2010-11 to the season before, and the form the simulator uses by build_season_sim.py\'s rule (leave-one-season-out log loss within those seasons; it picked ratings + last season + back-to-backs every time); the simulator runs 10,000 times at opening day, halfway and about 60 games in, against the record carried forward (log5) and the current standings. Player impact, 2022-23 to 2025-26 (stints start in 2020-21): ratings of the season before predict every tracked game margin, and every λ, prior scale and BPM / on/off scale is chosen by paper_eval\'s criterion on the pairs of seasons finished before the target (one pair for 2022-23 up to four for 2025-26); the Rating Tracker\'s five settings are re-estimated per season with its own estimator; the three-season window needs a full window to tune on, so it starts in 2024-25. The same ratings are also scored per possession (round 6\'s possessions table): the offence\'s predicted points from the ten on the floor when the possession began, times last season\'s points per counted possession over points per estimated possession (about 1.02, the same for every model). Shot model, 2021-22 to 2025-26: Shot Value Added\'s look-ahead-free prices (location model fitted on earlier seasons; plus the shooter\'s skill before the game) against last season\'s league FG% and FG% by shot class. Each season: every model\'s score with a 95% cluster bootstrap interval (games; team-seasons for the simulator; 10,000 resamples, one set shared by every model in the season) and every pair\'s difference with its interval, standard error and a sign-flip p. Pooled across seasons: DerSimonian-Laird random effects (τ = how much the true difference itself moves between seasons), the Hartung-Knapp interval (t with k − 1 degrees of freedom, never narrower than DerSimonian-Laird\'s), and a prediction interval for next season (t with k − 2). A flip is a season whose difference points the other way from the pooled one.',
                checked: 'Checked 2026-10-02 (report_card_pooled). The rolling run reproduces the protocol where they coincide: 2024-25 and 2025-26 pre-game odds equal paper_eval\'s to 3e-15, every impact model\'s 2024-25 game margins exactly (same tune pairs), the 2024-25 Rating Tracker re-estimate returns the stored settings, and the shot prices\' log loss equals Shot Value\'s validation table. Pre-game (14 seasons, log loss): blended ratings + back-to-backs beat Luck & Schedule\'s projection in all 14 seasons (−0.0113, 95% −0.0152 to −0.0074; clearly in 10), this season\'s ratings alone in 13 (−0.0099), and the back-to-back terms add −0.0017 (13 of 14): rankings that hold, and the next-season prediction intervals exclude zero. Simulator, halfway (playoff Brier): the model and the record carried forward split the seasons 7-7, pooled +0.0010 (−0.0096 to +0.0115), 7 flips: the protocol\'s record-wins-on-recent-seasons result is one side of a coin that keeps turning. The model\'s win totals are better at halfway (mean absolute error −0.25 wins, −0.49 to −0.01, 9 of 14 seasons) and its 80% ranges cover 15 points more teams. Player impact (game-margin RMSE, 4 seasons): BPM vs RAPM with a BPM prior 2-2, pooled +0.03 points (−0.37 to +0.44) with τ 0.23 and I² 87%: the protocol\'s reversal (prior ahead on tune and validate, BPM on test) is between-season variation, and next season could go either way by over a point. The Rating Tracker is ahead of BPM in 3 of 4 seasons (+0.13, −0.10 to +0.36, p 0.17) and of RAPM with a prior in 3 of 4; the prior beats one-season RAPM in all 4 (−0.20, −0.29 to −0.11). Shooter-aware expected-points RAPM with a prior is behind BPM in all 4 (+0.20, −0.03 to +0.42). Per possession the same order sharpens: the tracker beats BPM in 4 of 4 (p 0.02) and RAPM with a prior in 4 of 4 (p 0.003). Shot model: the shooter-aware price beats the shooter-blind one in all 5 seasons (−0.0027 log loss, −0.0031 to −0.0024), and both beat the baselines every season.',
